--TEST--
An `Export` whose rows are an `Iterator` writes its file once, and a second write throws through every method, while an export of an array or an `Iterable` writes the same file every time: rule:core-classes/spreadsheet-writes-as-a-stream
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Novis\Spreadsheet\Workbook::fromRows
// covers: Novis\Spreadsheet\Export::bytes
// covers: Novis\Spreadsheet\Export::to
use Novis\Spreadsheet\Workbook;

final class Orders implements Iterable<array<mixed>>
{
    public function iterate(): Iterator<array<mixed>>
    {
        return Rows::orders();
    }
}

final class Rows
{
    // This generator gives two rows.
    public static function orders(): Iterator<array<mixed>>
    {
        for (int $order = 1042; $order < 1044; $order++) {
            yield ["order" => $order];
        }
    }
}

var $once = Workbook::fromRows(Rows::orders(), {format: "csv"});
echo $once->bytes() as string;
try {
    $once->bytes();
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
Core\IO\File $file = Core\IO::open("orders.csv", Core\IO::FileMode::Write);
try {
    $once->to($file);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo "to throws too\n";
}
$file->close();

// An array and an `Iterable` give their rows again for each write.
var $array = Workbook::fromRows([["order" => 1042], ["order" => 1043]], {format: "csv"});
echo $array->bytes() == $array->bytes() ? "the array writes twice\n" : "the array differs\n";
var $iterable = Workbook::fromRows(new Orders(), {format: "csv"});
echo $iterable->bytes() == $iterable->bytes() ? "the iterable writes twice\n" : "the iterable differs\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
order
1042
1043
The rows of this export are an `Iterator`, and an iterator can be read only once. Call `Workbook::fromRows` again with new rows, or pass an array.
to throws too
the array writes twice
the iterable writes twice
